nL1193@P34: • OECD (2011), Towards Green Growth: Monitoring Progress: OECD Indicators, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD\nL1194@P34: Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264111356-en.\nL1195@P34: • OECD (2008), Measuring Material Flows and Resource Productivity.\nL1196@P34: http://www.oecd.org/environment/resourceefficiency.\nL1197@P34: • OECD (2001), Sustainable development: Critical issues, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL1198@P34: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264193185-en.\nL1569@P45: 6 www.step-initiative.org/index.php/overview-world.html\nL1570@P45: REFERENCES\nL1571@P45: • EEA (European Environment Agency) (2010), The European Environment: State and Outlook 2010 –Material\nL1572@P45: Resources and Waste. www.eea.europa.eu/soer/europe/material-resources-and-waste.\nL1573@P45: • OECD (2014), Green Growth Indicators 2014, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL1574@P45: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264202030-en.\nL1575@P45: • OECD (2011), Towards Green Growth: Monitoring Progress: OECD Indicators, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD\nL1576@P45: Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264111356-en.\nL1577@P45: • OECD (2011), Resource Productivity in the G8 and the OECD: A Report in the Framework of the Kobe 3R Action Plan,\nL1578@P45: ENV/EPOC/WPRPW/WPEI(2011)1/FINAL, OECD, Paris, http://www.oecd.org/env/waste/47944428.pdf.\nL1579@P45: • OECD (2008a), Recommendation of the Council on Resource Productivity [C(2008)40], OECD, Paris, available at\nL1580@P45: http://webnet.oecd.org/oecdacts/.\nL1581@P45: • OECD (2008b), Measuring Material Flows and Resource Productivity.\nL1832@P52: MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 51\nL1833@P52: Box 3.1. OECD references to material flows and resource productivity issues\nL1834@P52: The Council of the OECD has adopted several policy and legal texts of relevance to sustainable resource\nL1835@P52: use and resource productivity and concerning actions that Member countries agreed to carry out in the\nL1836@P52: framework of the Organisation.\nL1837@P52: In 2001, OECD Environment Ministers and the OECD Council at ministerial level adopted the OECD\nL1838@P52: Environmental Strategy for the First decade of the 21st Century that includes two relevant\nL1839@P52: objectives: (i) maintaining the integrity of ecosystems through the efficient management of\nL1840@P52: natural resources; (ii) decoupling environmental pressure from economic growth and making\nL1841@P52: integrated efforts to address consumption and production patterns, including by encouraging\nL1842@P52: more efficient resource use and hence increases in resource productivity.\nL1843@P52: This follows on the recommendations formulated in 1997 of the High Level Advisory Group on the\nL1844@P52: Environment to the Secretary General of the OECD: \"… it is now time for the OECD to\nL1845@P52: concentrate on increasing resource productivity with the same effectiveness it applied to\nL1846@P52: labour productivity. This should be done not just for environmental reasons, but also for\nL1847@P52: economic and social reasons\" (OECD, 1997)\nL1848@P52: In 2004, the Council of the OECD adopted a Recommendation on material flows and resource\nL1854@P52: environmental impacts, and for measuring resource productivity, and (ii) to take actions to\nL1855@P52: improve resource productivity and reduce negative environmental impacts of materials and\nL1856@P52: product use, by encouraging environmentally effective and economically efficient uses of natural\nL1857@P52: resources and materials at the macro, sectoral and micro levels.\nL1858@P52: In 2011, the OECD green growth strategy called for policies that ”foster economic growth and\nL1859@P52: development while ensuring that the natural assets continue to provide the resources and\nL1860@P52: environmental services on which our well-being relies”.\nL1861@P52: In 2011, the Council of the OECD adopted a Recommendation on due diligence guidance for responsible\nL1862@P52: supply chains of minerals from confict-affected and high-risk areas. It provides detailed\nL1863@P52: recommendations to help companies respect human rights and avoid contributing to conflict\nL1864@P52: through their mineral purchasing decisions and practices. It is for use by any company potentially\nL1865@P52: sourcing minerals or metals from conflict-affected and high-risk areas, and is one of the only\nL1866@P52: international frameworks available to help companies meet their due diligence reporting\nL1867@P52-53: requirements.CHAPTER 3. SUSTAINABLE RESOURCE USE AND RESOURCE PRODUCTIVITY\nL1868@P53: 52 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL1869@P53: Box 3.2. Other International references to material flows and resource productivity issues\nL1870@P53: G8 summits\nL1871@P53: In 2003, the Heads of State and Government of G8 countries adopted an Action Plan on “Science and\nL1872@P53: Technology for Sustainable Development” stating that G8 members “will enhance their\nL1873@P53: understanding of resource material flows and continue work on resources productivity indices,\nL1874@P53: notably in the OECD”. (Evian summit, 1-3 June 2003, France). This followed on the communiqué by\nL1875@P53: G8 Environment Ministers who recognised “that it is essential to improve resource productivity” and\nL1876@P53: noted “that a common approach has to be elaborated in order to identify and develop indicators ... to\nL1877@P53: monitor the shift in consumption and production patterns”. They invited “the OECD to play a\nL1878@P53: supportive role in that respect”. (Communiqué adopted by G8 Environment Ministers, Paris, 25-27\nL1879@P53: April 2003).\nL1880@P53: In 2004, the Heads of State and Government of G8 countries endorsed the \"Reduce, Reuse and Recycle\"\nL1881@P53: (3Rs) initiative proposed by Japan as part of the follow-up to the Action Plan on “Science and\nL1882@P53: Technology for Sustainable Development” adopted in 2003. (Sea Island summit, 9-10 June 2004,\nL1883@P53: United States).\nL1884@P53: In 2006, the Heads of State and Government of G8 countries adopted a Plan of Action on Global Energy\nL1885@P53: Security emphasising the importance of energy efficiency and stating that \"As part of an integrated\nL1886@P53: approach to the entire resource cycle we reaffirm our commitment to comprehensive measures to\nL1887@P53: optimise the resource cycle within the 3Rs Initiative (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle). In furthering these\nL1888@P53: efforts, we will set targets as appropriate taking account of resource productivity.\" (St Petersburg\nL1889@P53: summit, 15-17 July 2006, Russia).\nL1890@P53: In 2007, the Heads of State and Government of G8 countries agreed to increase transparency and good\nL1891@P53: governance in the raw materials sector as a contribution to sustainable growth, and stated that \"based\nL1892@P53: on sound life cycle analyses, we will … encourage conservation, recycling and substitution of raw\nL1893@P53: materials, including rare metals, for sustainable growth.\" (Heiligendamm summit, 6-8 June 2007,\nL1894@P53: Germany).\nL1895@P53: In 2008, the Heads of State and Government of G8 countries endorsed the Kobe 3R Action Plan in which G8\nL1896@P53: Environment Ministers agreed to take action to improve resource productivity, establish an\nL1897@P53: international sound material-cycle society, and collaborate for 3R capacity development in developing\nL1898@P53: countries. The Action Plan requests the OECD to follow up on the progress of work related to resource\nL1899@P53: productivity (Kobe, 24-26 May 2008, Japan).\nL1900@P53: European Union references\nL1901@P53: In 2005, the European Union adopted a thematic strategy on the sustainable use of natural resources,\nL1902@P53: complemented with a strategy on the prevention and recycling of waste, and with integrated product\nL1903@P53: policies (IPP) aiming at improving environmental performance of products through their full life\u0002cycle. Other EU texts of relevance include an Environmental Technology Action plan, and a\nL1904@P53: Sustainable Consumption and Production and Sustainable Industrial Policy Action Plan (2008).\nL1905@P53: The EU Raw Materials Initiative (November 2008) and Strategy (February 2011) set out measures to secure\nL1906@P53: access to non-energy raw materials for the EU, to boost resource efficiency and promote recycling\nL1907@P53: including through improvements in recycling markets, in waste treatment, in statistics on waste and\nL1908@P53: materials flows.\nL1909@P53: These objectives are also part of the EU 2020 Flagship Initiative on Resource Efficiency (January 2011) that\nL1910@P53: includes a Resource Efficiency Roadmap specifying policy goals and targets, and indicators to\nL1911@P53: measure progress.\nL1912@P53: United Nations\nL1913@P53: The UN Conference on Sustainable Development in June 2012 (Rio+20), reaffirmed that changing\nL1914@P53: unsustainable consumption and production patterns is one of the three overarching objectives of, and\nL1915@P53: essential requirements for, sustainable development, and adopted the 10-Year Framework of\nL1916@P53: Programmes on Sustainable Consumption and Production Patterns (10YFP). UNEP acts as\nL2287@P60: 4 For instance, the green growth strategy of Chile; the circular economy roadmap in France; and the Green\nL2288@P60: Economy Action Plan in Switzerland.\nL2289@P60: 5 For instance, the United States Sustainable Manufacturing Initiative (SMI) and Public-Private Dialogue\nL2290@P60: launched by the Department of Commerce to support United States companies in their sustainable\nL2291@P60: manufacturing efforts; the Material-efficiency Centre in Finland; or the Resource efficiency network set up\nL2292@P60: by the German Ministry of the Environment.\nL2293@P60: REFERENCES\nL2294@P60: • European Commission, Resource Efficiency,\nL2295@P60: http://ec.europa.eu/environment/resource_efficiency/about/index_en.htm\nL2296@P60: • European Commission, Sustainable use of natural resources, http://ec.europa.eu/environment/natres/index.htm\nL2297@P60: • EEA (European Environment Agency ) (2010), The European Environment: State and Outlook 2010 –Material\nL2298@P60: Resources and Waste, www.eea.europa.eu/soer/europe/material-resources-and-waste.\nL2299@P60: • OECD (2014), Green Growth Indicators 2014, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL2300@P60: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264202030-en.\nL2301@P60: • OECD (2011) Resource Productivity in the G8 and OECD: A Report in the Framework of the Kobe 3R Action Plan,\nL2302@P60: www.oecd.org/document/14/0,3746,en_2649_34395_47926478_1_1_1_1,00.html.\nL2303@P60: • OECD (2011), Towards Green Growth: Monitoring Progress: OECD Indicators, OECD Green Growth Studies, OECD\nL2304@P60: Publishing, Paris, http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264111356-en.\nL2305@P60: • OECD (2008), Recommendation of the Council on Resource Productivity [C(2008)40], OECD, Paris, available at\nL2306@P60: available at http://webnet.oecd.org/oecdacts/.\nL2307@P60: • OECD (2008b), Measuring Material Flows and Resource Productivity.\nL2308@P60: www.oecd.org/environment/resourceefficiency.\nL2309@P60: • OECD (2008c), OECD Environmental Outlook to 2030, OECD Publishing, Paris,\nL2310@P60: http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264040519-en.\nL2311@P60: • OECD (2004), Recommendation of the Council on Material Flows and Resource Productivity [C(2004)79], Paris,\nL2663@P68: The indicator UDE in the OECD material flow database does not account for soil erosion. Estimating soil\nL2664@P68: erosion is complex and beyond the scope of this report.\nL2665@P68: 740\nL2666@P68: 848\nL2667@P68: 953\nL2668@P68: 438\nL2669@P68: 496\nL2670@P68: 584\nL2671@P68: 0\nL2672@P68: 200\nL2673@P68: 400\nL2674@P68: 600\nL2675@P68: 800\nL2676@P68: 1000\nL2677@P68: 1200\nL2678@P68: 1980 1990 2010\nL2679@P68: GDP per unit of DEU\nL2680@P68: GDP per unit of DEU+UDE\nL2681@P68: 1000 USD per tonne\